A||||7||Greg Piet||Junkyard Engine advice||||1||
Z||000000||Greg Piet||12-30-1999||05:40 PM||||I just went to the boneyard and for the first time I found a v6 in a fiero.  The cars engine has no oil on it, and there are no holes apparent from an engine explosion, but the engine is really dusty.  <P>The boneyard wants 200 for the longblock and I have to pull it out.  Does this sound like a good idea(I can buy a rebuilt motor for a grand right)?  Are there certain things I should look for before doing this?  <P>The car also has the extra pod guage thing (which I dont really need/want) but does someone else want me to try to pull it out?  What price? What stuff do you need out of it besides the center gauges/housing?  (the dash gauges are screwed up)  The radio pod looked like it had a differnt form on the top for the gauge pod to rest on top.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-30-1999||08:07 PM||||The "extra pod gauge thing" is the Rally gauges and get those for sure (hopefully they work!).  As for the motor, I don't think you could get a brand new, complete motor for a grand.  If so, let us know where.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill Osmer||12-30-1999||08:28 PM||||You should try to get the radio, too. You didn't mention whether there was one or whether it might be a premium version. Check for a subwoofer and the overhead lamp assembly w/subwoofer control - these are in big demand, too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RFMike||12-30-1999||09:44 PM||||As I have posted before, a long block 2.8 can be ordered from Big Wheel for $967, which includes shipping.  $300 core charge.  <BR>.040 overbore is standard.  Performance cam is $120 upcharge.  3.1 crank is $170. This does include the Fiero HP heads.  3 year/ 36,000 mile warranty.  I am probably going to go this route if or when I wear the original engine out.  I know you don't have as much rebuild control, but this would close to what I am looking for.  I drive this car too many ordinary highway miles to go completely nuts on performance parts.<BR>Incidentally, there many remanufacturers out there.  If anybody knows the best ones, or the ones to stay away from, please post.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||12-30-1999||10:13 PM||||OK First of all Everybody on this site ROCKS!<BR>RFMike could you give me some contact info for that engine place.<P>Ill check for the "performance sound" system (i forgot to check before since i already got one)<P>What else is needed for the ralley gauge setup?  A wiring harness i've been told, and what else? (and is the radio pod different for a ralley gauge setup?)  <P>I think with the work involved in removing the motor and the uncertainty of how messed up it is i thik the 1000 dollar longblock motor is a safer bet for me with the facilities available to me.<P>Thanks all again and ill try to help all my fellow fierophiles!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||12-30-1999||10:47 PM||||The radio pod has a cut out section on the top.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RFMike||12-31-1999||06:44 PM||||Greg,<BR>Big Wheel is a parts store chain.  They are a distributor for several engine remans.  That's why I'm looking for a recommendation.  The info I posted is for a Recon engine.  Recon has its own web site, and their staff seems to be well informed.  Big Wheel also distributes Jasper engines, for a little more money, but a longer warranty (75K/ 36 months, for those of you who pile on the miles).  They handle a couple of others, but these two had the best prices.<BR>If you do a Web search, you will find dozens of remanufacturers throughout the country.  This is a big step, with a lot of trust involved.  Before I take it, I need feedback about the company I go with.  Anybody?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||01-03-2000||02:25 PM||||I've heard of Jasper engines.  They build a lot of hipo motors.  <P>You should also as around  at the local machine shops.  Some sell rebuilt short and long blocks and an upgrade to a hipo cam and some port work may only be a little extra.||||reg||1
